dzn: Create constant buffer views for shader-visible heaps through a copy

NVIDIA's D3D12 driver under WSL2 takes 4.1 us to create a constant buffer
view in a shader-visible descriptor heap, against 0.015 us in a CPU-only
heap; copying one descriptor from a CPU-only heap into a shader-visible one
takes 0.15 us (RTX 4080 Laptop GPU, driver 595.79, 20000 views each). On
the same laptop's Intel UHD Graphics every one of these takes under 0.01 us.
Without bindless descriptors Dozen creates such a view for each dynamic
uniform buffer on every draw that follows new dynamic offsets, which is how
ANGLE passes uniforms: a WebGL page making 6000 draws a frame spent 80% of
a host core in the driver's view creation and drew 12 frames a second.

Create constant buffer views meant for a shader-visible heap in a one-slot
CPU-only heap and copy them across, under a lock. Views for CPU-only heaps
are created in place as before.

Android 16 on the RTX 4080 under WSL2, two runs each: the WebGL page at
6000 draws a frame 42.9 frames a second instead of 12.0, at 12000 draws
28.6 instead of 7.1, at 1500 and 3000 draws 54 instead of 38 and 24.

